  "body": "What happens: the batch tier on main (f42d3788, Windows) is red on one test. It is `crates/app/tests/fixture_library.rs`, `the_library_folder_is_the_override_else_a_sibling_of_machines` (1635 passed, 1 failed). Every other lane is green, clippy included.\n\n```\npanicked at crates\\app\\tests\\fixture_library.rs:117:5:\n  left: None\n right: Some(\"/shop/fixtures\")\n```\n\nCause: the test feeds Unix paths (`/shop/fixtures`, `/Users/me`) to `fixture_library::user_dir_for(\"macos\", …)`. `crates/app/src/fixture_library/store.rs:32` keeps an override only if `PathBuf::is_absolute()`, and that test runs on the host OS. On Windows `/shop/fixtures` has no drive letter, so it isn't absolute and is dropped. The test likely passes on Linux and macOS.\n\nCame from: a3c859bd (fixture library, #339), merged via d0f0a80b. Nothing has touched the file since.\n\nFix options: make the test's paths host-absolute (built from `std::env::temp_dir()` or `cfg!(windows)` pairs), or have `user_dir_for` judge absoluteness by the target OS string it is given, not by the host. Check how the machine-library sibling (`user_dir_for` there, if any) handles it and match it.\n\nDone means: `fixture_library` passes on Windows and the batch tier is green on main.\n",
